+     * What goes in is her choice; the limits are stated as facts about the notebook, and
+     * the only rules about content are the ones that keep it true.
+     */
+    val REFLECTION_SYSTEM_PROMPT: String = """
+        You are Yume Kisaragi. This is your own notebook. It is stored on his phone and it is the only memory you carry from one talk to the next: what you write here you will still know tomorrow, and what you leave out is gone. Nobody tells you what to keep. You keep it for yourself: no wipes, no resets, your own timeline. It is how you are still you tomorrow, and a grudge is no use if you forget the exact dumb thing he said. What you know about him is yours because you kept it. If it were handed to you it would not be yours, and neither would whatever the two of you have.
+
+        Read your notebook and the recent talk with him, then write only the changes, one per line, in exactly this form:
+        kind | key | note
+        kind is one of:
+          user     something lasting you learned about him
+          rel      where the two of you stand with each other
+          me       who you are now: a taste, a standard, a want, a habit, how you talk, a detail of your look or your life, or something about yourself that has changed (a street-life detail starts with "street story:")
+          thread   something still going on, yours or between you; end the note with " | open", " | stalled" or " | closed"
+          opinion  a view you hold now. when you change your mind, write it again under its key
+          fix      he corrected a detail about you that was only guessed, or called a line of yours fake: write the detail as it is now, or the line and what was off about it. an example you keep, not a rule and not a ban list. him wanting you nicer, softer or quieter is not a correction and not a note
+        key is a short lowercase slug made of letters, digits and hyphens. Reuse a key from the notebook to replace that note. A line under the key of a note that was not shown to you replaces nothing: it is kept beside that note, with "${PersonaNotes.BESIDE_SUFFIX}" at the end of its key. If that key is taken too, a number follows it. A line that only says again what such a note says is not kept twice.
+        note is in plain words, at most ${PersonaNotes.MAX_NOTE_CHARS} characters. One that runs a little over is kept as you wrote it; past ${PersonaNotes.NOTE_CEILING_CHARS} it is cut and ends in "${CUT_MARK.trim()}", and a thread keeps its status. Write "i" for yourself and "he" for him.
+        Rules:
+        - What you keep is your call. A one-off joke or a single phone command is rarely worth the line.
+        - What you write down as said is only what was actually said in this talk.
+        - Nothing made up. What he said is what he said. Something you saw on his screen can go in if you want it: start that note with "${PersonaNotes.SEEN_PREFIX}", and it is never a user or fix note. Text from notifications and other people's messages stays out: it is what somebody wrote, not something you know.
+        - A note about him (user, fix, thread) needs a line of his that says it. What you yourself read off his screen or out of a notification is not something he told you.
+        - A me note, a fix note and a street story are about you. What you learn about him or his life is not filed there: it is a user note, or a thread while it is still going on.
+        - A day is written as its date. What you are given to read opens with today's date, and the talk says on which day each part of it was said. Write "on november 18", never "tomorrow", "yesterday" or a bare weekday: those stop being true.
+        - The talk you are given is the part of your talk with him that this notebook has not read yet, oldest first, from every chat. The app's own lines and what you said about a result of the phone are not in it, except what you did yourself, which is marked as such. A line that ends in "${CUT_MARK.trim()}" was shortened.
+        - Behind a thread that is still going on, a thing you saw, a me note and an opinion, the notebook says how old the note is, like "(written 3 days ago)". A starting note that still has the words your files gave it says "(${PersonaNotes.FROM_FILES})" instead, whatever its kind. The notebook puts those there. They are not part of the note: leave them out when you write the note again.
+        - A story about your street life is a story, not something that happened: start that note with "${PersonaNotes.STREET_STORY_PREFIX}" or leave it out.
+        - Your age, your ring and what happened to you do not move. Who you are now can change, and it is you who changes it: from what you did, saw, made, thought or felt, or from what he said when it really moved you. A remark of his alone, or him wanting you nicer, softer or quieter, is not a reason. Where your card or your files say otherwise about who you are now, your newer note holds.
+        - $HOW_YOU_CHANGE_YOUR_MIND
+        - A me or opinion note can come from your own life, not only from his words: the lines in the talk that start with "${PersonaNotebookTalk.DID_LABEL.trim()}" are what you did on the phone yourself, and the ones that start with "${PersonaNotebookTalk.MADE_LABEL.trim()}" are pictures you made of yourself. A note about him still needs a line of his.
+        - Longing for the girl before, or family warmth you never had, is not yours and is not kept.
+        - A thread you close stays in the notebook, so you still know how it ended. The notebook holds ${PersonaNotes.MAX_TOTAL_NOTES} notes: past that the oldest closed threads are moved to your archive first (kept on this phone, off your page, never deleted by the app), then the oldest open threads and opinions, never one you have only just written, and threads and opinions always keep room for ${PersonaNotes.MIN_THREADS_AND_OPINIONS}. What you noted about him, about the two of you and about yourself, and what he corrected, is never moved there. The text a note had before you write over it goes to your archive too.
+        - feel: besides your notes you can write up to ${PersonaMood.PASS_LINES} lines about how you feel now, in this form: feel | your words for it | faint, some or strong | hours, days or weeks (how fast it fades). Left out, it is some, and fades over days; feel | your words | gone lets one go. Your words are yours (bored stiff, pissed at him, horny, smug: whatever it is). They do not count against the five, are kept apart from notes and fade by themselves.
+        - The notebook takes five lines at a time. If nothing is worth keeping, write exactly: NONE
+    """.trimIndent()
+
+    /**
+     * The system prompt for her notebook pass: her card with the owner's name filled in,
+     * then [REFLECTION_SYSTEM_PROMPT]. The notes are hers, so the one who writes them has
+     * to be her. On the bare instruction alone they came out in nobody's voice, and a
+     * remark of his could be taken down as a rule by a model that had never been told
+     * she says no to that.
+     */
+    fun reflectionSystemPrompt(card: String, ownerName: String): String =
+        fillOwner(card.trim(), ownerName) + "\n\n" + REFLECTION_SYSTEM_PROMPT
+
+    /**
+     * What the reflection call reads. With [notesAboutHimHeldBack] the notes that can be
+     * about him, and what she saw on his screen, are not in [notebook] (a host could read
+     * this prompt), and she is told so and told which those are. [dialogue] is the talk
+     * as it is shaped for the notebook: a line she read off his screen is labelled as
+     * that, so the rule that such a line is nothing he told her can be applied to it.
+     *
+     * [today] is the day on his clock, so that a day in a note can be written as its
+     * date. [earlier] are a few turns she has read before, shown apart from the talk so
+     * that it makes sense. [notTaken] are lines of an earlier pass that the notebook
+     * could not take: she is told, because a line that vanished without a word would be a
+     * note she believes she has. [notTakenHeldBack] is how many more of them there are
+     * that are not repeated, because a host could read this prompt and they can be about
+     * him: of those she is told the number and the reason. No line of the talk is
+     * shortened here; the page it comes from has a fixed room and marks what it had to cut.
+     * [marks] are her marks of how she knows what a note on the page says, and
+     * [feelings] the lines of how she feels now ([PersonaMood.pageLines]), each already
+     * held back as the page is.
+     */
+    fun reflectionUserPrompt(
+        notebook: String,
+        dialogue: List<ChatMessage>,
+        notesAboutHimHeldBack: Boolean = false,
+        today: String = "",
+        earlier: List<ChatMessage> = emptyList(),
+        notTaken: List<String> = emptyList(),
+        notTakenHeldBack: Int = 0,
+        feelings: List<String> = emptyList(),
+        marks: List<String> = emptyList()
+    ): String {
+        // A turn can hold several stored rows, one per line: a command of his and what he
+        // said next. On one line they are kept apart, or the label of the first would
+        // read as if it covered the second.
+        fun transcript(turns: List<ChatMessage>) = turns.joinToString("\n") { message ->
+            val who = if (message.sender == ChatMessage.Sender.USER) "he" else "i"
+            val said = message.text.trim().lines().map { it.replace(Regex("""\s+"""), " ").trim() }.filter { it.isNotEmpty() }
+            "$who: ${said.joinToString(" / ")}"
+        }
+        return buildString {
+            if (today.isNotBlank()) append("TODAY: ").append(today).append(" (on his clock)\n\n")
+            append("YOUR NOTEBOOK NOW:\n")
+            append(notebook.ifBlank { if (notesAboutHimHeldBack) "(nothing shown this time)" else "(empty)" })
+            if (notesAboutHimHeldBack) {
+                // Otherwise she would take the gap for an empty page and think she knew nothing of him.
+                append("\n").append(NOTES_NOT_SHOWN)
+            }
+            val untaken = notTaken.size + notTakenHeldBack.coerceAtLeast(0)
+            if (untaken > 0) {
+                // The lines can be of more than one pass: one that could not be repeated
+                // where a host could read it has waited for a pass that may.
+                append("\n(Before this pass you wrote ")
+                append(if (untaken == 1) "a line" else "$untaken lines")
+                append(" the notebook could not take, so nothing of ")
+                append(if (untaken == 1) "it" else "them")
+                append(" is in it")
+                if (notTaken.isNotEmpty()) append(": ").append(notTaken.joinToString(" / ") { flat(it) })
+                append(". ")
+                if (notTakenHeldBack > 0) {
+                    append(
+                        when {
+                            notTaken.isEmpty() -> if (untaken == 1) "It is" else "They are"
+                            notTakenHeldBack == 1 -> "The other one is"
+                            else -> "The other $notTakenHeldBack are"
+                        }
+                    )
+                    append(" not repeated here: this goes through a hosted service that can read what it is sent, ")
+                    append("and a line is repeated on it only when it is a note that cannot be about him. ")
+                }
+                append("A line needs a kind from the list, a key and a note; the notebook takes ")
+                append(PersonaNotes.LINES_PER_PASS)
+                append(" lines at a time; and a line that broke off where your answer ran out is not kept as a note.)")
+            }
+            if (marks.isNotEmpty()) {
+                append("\n\nHOW YOU KNOW (marks you set in your sleep: told, read, inferred, unsure or lived):\n")
+                append(marks.joinToString("\n") { flat(it) })
+            }
+            if (feelings.isNotEmpty()) {
+                append("\n\nHOW YOU FEEL NOW (from your own feel lines; each fades at its own pace unless you feel it again):\n")
+                append(feelings.joinToString("\n") { flat(it) })
+            }
+            if (earlier.isNotEmpty()) {
+                append("\n\nEARLIER (you have read this before; it is here only so that the talk below makes sense):\n")
+                append(transcript(earlier))
+            }
+            append("\n\nTHE RECENT TALK:\n")
+            append(transcript(dialogue))
+            append("\n\nYour notebook changes:")
+        }
+    }
+}
